INT. BAIT SHOP - DAWN

Minnow tanks bubble in the half-dark. A radio on the counter plays low static. The smell of brine, worms, and old coffee. Pale light creeps through a salt-fogged window.

LENNOX BARRETT (50s, the owner, flannel shirt, a thick scar through one eyebrow) stands behind the counter with a landline receiver clamped between ear and shoulder. His other hand rests flat on a lockbox. He has not slept. The clock on the wall says 5:42.

MAMA PEARL (V.O.)
(over phone)
You promised me, Lennox. You promised you'd be home before the sun came up.

LENNOX
I know I did, Mama.

MAMA PEARL (V.O.)
(over phone)
I made grits. I put the pan back on the stove twice. Your sister's been here since four. She says there's a man on the dock.

LENNOX
There's always a man on the dock.

MAMA PEARL (V.O.)
(over phone)
Not like this one. She says he's been standing there since midnight with his hat in his hands.

Lennox looks out the window. The dock is empty except for a gull. He turns back and lifts the lid of the lockbox, and the stacks of bills inside catch the light like a pale fan.

LENNOX
Mama, listen to me. I'm going to put this in a bag, and I'm going to carry it out to the truck. I want you to put the phone down and go sit with Ruthie.

MAMA PEARL (V.O.)
(over phone)
Lennox, the money isn't the thing. It never was. Your father used to say a man is as much what he gives back as what he keeps.

LENNOX
Daddy never kept a thing in his life.

MAMA PEARL (V.O.)
(over phone)
That's the point, baby. That's exactly the point.

He closes the box. He runs his thumb along the latch, once, and his shoulders drop.

LENNOX
Put the pan back on, Mama. Make it hot.

MAMA PEARL (V.O.)
(over phone)
I never took it off.

He hangs up. He carries the lockbox out through the beaded door, across the planks, to the end of the dock. The sun lifts the edge of the water.
